  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 420 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe ridging feature that dominated Eastern Kentucky's sensible \nweather during the short term forecast period is expected to erode\nas the long term period progresses. A series of shortwave \nimpulses navigating around the ridge's northeastern periphery and \na closed upper level low pushing west across the Floridian \nPeninsula will work in tandem to suppress the ridge this weekend. \nIt is progged to weaken to a loosely-organized, sub-590 decameter \nridge over the Ozarks by Monday, a far cry from its peak intensity\nas a well-defined 600+ decameter \"heat dome.\" As this weakening \nprocess occurs, the forecast area will be positioned in somewhat \nof a split flow regime. The stronger westerlies associated with \nthe jet stream will remain displaced well to the north of the Ohio\nRiver through early next week, and as the ridge loses its \ndefinition, the winds aloft will weaken and become more quasi-\nzonal. So, this weekend's sensible weather forecast will largely \nbe governed by smaller scale features and processes. \n\nSome of those mesoscale forecast ingredients are hard to predict at \nthe current temporal range. Machine learning and AI-enhanced \nprobabilistic models are beginning to resolve a corridor of low-end \nsevere storm potential along the NE periphery of the decaying ridge. \nThis will need to be investigated further as convection-allowing, \nhigher-resolution models enter the forecast guidance suite. The weak \nnature of the flow aloft will limit the magnitude of the shear \navailable for any storms developing within our CWA this weekend, but \nif a self-sustaining MCS develops somewhere upstream, we will need \nto monitor its progress. This would cause the forecast to deviate \nfrom current expectations, which are more scattered, pulse-type \nconvection during peak diurnal heating hours. Any MCS could also \nthrow a wrench in the temperature forecast, which currently calls \nfor highs in the upper 80s/lower 90s on both Saturday and Sunday. A \nfew isolated hydro impacts cannot be entirely ruled out where \nmultiple rounds of activity occur this weekend, and WPC has \naccordingly outlined Marginal Excessive Rainfall Outlooks that clip \nthe northern Licking and northeastern Big Sandy River Basins.\n\nThere is still a notable increase in model spread within the NBM \nensemble across multiple sensible weather forecast parameters for \nMonday and beyond. This is likely due to differing model solutions \nsurrounding a deeper trough ejecting out of the Plains. Significant \nvariations in this feature's modeled amplitude, orientation, \nphasing, and forward progression speed continue to preclude the \ndiscussion of specific precipitation forecast details, but models\ngenerally agree that a deeper, vertically-stacked trough will \nmove through the Midwest and the Greater Ohio River Valley by \nmidweek. It would take a trough like this to finally drag a \nmeaningful cold front through the CWA. So, despite the model \nspread, there is a noticeable downward trend in the mean NBM \nforecast temperatures. Highs could dip down into the low/mid 80s \nby Tuesday, and then the 70s on Wednesday and Thursday. Northern \nand Northeastern portions of the forecast area are the most likely\nto reap the benefits of this cool- down, and the cooler air will \nalso reach them first. Thus, the gradual demise of the ridge could\ngive our area a taste of more fall- esque temperatures by the \nmiddle of the next work week. Fingers crossed, as this would be \nwelcome relief from the record high temperature observed at our \noffice while this discussion was being typed.",
